{
  "id": "ep140",
  "duration": 55.71,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.825,
      "text": "A giant moon of Saturn vanished every time it orbited east.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Iapetus colorized.jpg",
        "credit": "Unknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetus_colorized.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.825,
      "end": 13.67,
      "text": "In 1671, astronomer Giovanni Domenico Cassini tracked Iapetus on Saturn's western side, but lost it entirely on the east.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Giovanni Domenico Cassini, Biblioteca Aprosiana.jpg",
        "credit": "Unknown authorUnknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Domenico_Cassini,_Biblioteca_Aprosiana.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.833,
          "end": 8.733,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 8.733,
          "end": 13.667,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 13.67,
      "end": 20.165,
      "text": "Cassini deduced that Iapetus was tidally locked, with one bright hemisphere and one dark hemisphere.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Color hemispheres map of Iapetus PIA18436 Nov. 2014.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA / JPL-Caltech / Space Science Institute / Lunar and Planetary Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Color_hemispheres_map_of_Iapetus_PIA18436_Nov._2014.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 13.667,
          "end": 16.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 16.133,
          "end": 20.167,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.165,
      "end": 27.335,
      "text": "Modern spacecraft proved him right: its trailing side gleams bright ice, while its leading face is coal-black.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Iapetusnorth.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetusnorth.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.167,
          "end": 24.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 24.133,
          "end": 27.333,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 27.335,
      "end": 36.23,
      "text": "Because Iapetus takes 79 days to rotate, thermal runaway strips ice into colder zones, leaving dark soot just one foot thick.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Inky stains on a frozen moon.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inky_stains_on_a_frozen_moon.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 27.333,
          "end": 36.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.23,
      "end": 43.5,
      "text": "Then, in 2004, the Cassini probe revealed an equatorial mountain ridge towering 12 miles high.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Iapetus mountains Larger.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetus_mountains_Larger.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.233,
          "end": 43.5,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.5,
      "end": 50.35,
      "text": "This colossal mountain wall and bulging waistline give the two-toned moon the bizarre shape of a walnut.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Iapetus.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA / JPL-Caltech / Space Science Institute / Cassini Orbiter",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetus.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.5,
          "end": 50.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 50.35,
      "end": 55.36,
      "text": "How would you explain a world that goes completely invisible on one side of its orbit?",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Iapetus colorized.jpg",
        "credit": "Unknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapetus_colorized.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 50.367,
          "end": 55.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
